A||||30||LZeitgeist||EDUCATED OPINIONS WANTED - Ram Clutch - Fiero Store||||1||
Z||000000||LZeitgeist||11-16-2001||03:10 PM||||O.K., it's time to replace the P.O.S. Advance Auto no-name clutch I put in my Formula last year. <P>Looks like the choice is boiling down to the Ram Heavy-Duty Clutch (not the racing version) from the Fiero Store. I *think* this is the one we put in Formula88's Formula last year (please correct me if I'm wrong), so I'm hoping to find out how he still likes it, as well as hopefully get some pros and cons from the rest of you who might be running this clutch with your V-6/5-speed.<P>Can anyone help me decide if this is truly the way to go?<P>Thanks in advance...<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show<P>EDIT - damn typos...<p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 11-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||11-16-2001||03:16 PM||||Yup.  Ram HD clutch, not the "Powergrip" Kevlar Racing clutch.  It's got about 20K miles of hard driving on it and seems ok.  Not a tight as it was at first, but I guess that's normal wear and tear.  No noticeable slippage.<P>I consider it an stock quality replacement.  I would probably look for something heavier-duty for a modded or NOS'ed engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LZeitgeist||11-16-2001||03:24 PM||||Another question I forgot to include - (for everyone else as well as Formula88):<P>Is there another clutch you wish you'd used, or would you go with the Ram H-D again for a stock application?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-16-2001||05:01 PM||||I put the RAM HD clutch set in my Formula in winter of '99. It has around 30K daily driver miles on it. I have hated it from day one and will not buy another. There are other threads on general chat that you can search for "RAM" and they will come up with what I and as it turns out several others have all had the same experience. It slips when warm, it won't engauge smooth when cold, etc...<P>Run a search, I would but don't have the time to do it right now.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||11-16-2001||05:05 PM||||Well, I haven't run into that problem.  I do drive the car hard, but don't do clutch dumps very often.  I try to be relatively easy on the clutch.<P>If I had it to do over, I'd probably consider Archie's Centerforce clutch, but it's a bit more expensive.<BR> <BR>If you didn't like the RAM, what have you used that you liked better?||||reg||1
Z||000005||LZeitgeist||11-16-2001||05:08 PM||||Every search I run on clutches comes up with a pile full of threads telling people looking for info to do a search. <P>Thanks for your steer-clear info, though... I'll definitely keep it in mind...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Master_Sushi||11-16-2001||06:12 PM||||Whats the OEM or stock clutch like compared to the "ram" clutches? Does it hold much more then stock? I have heard some good and some bad about the RAM's, but I never drove one myself so I won't take sides.||||reg||5
Z||000007||fiero56||11-16-2001||06:12 PM||||I'll try to find it tonight. It was titled something like "for those with RAM clutch and 2.8". I'm at work right now, and haven't figured out how to use the new search yet, so I'm useless, I tried already. 1FST2M6 had the same experience I had.<P>I am still living with it for another month or so. I hate it, but have a 2.8 build in progress. I will be using a CenterforceI clutch set this time around. Hopefully it is much nicer. It is more costly though, I think I paid around $350 for the set. I'll have to check receipts.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||11-16-2001||06:14 PM||||Found it:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20010924-1-006281.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20010924-1-006281.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||avengador1||11-16-2001||06:25 PM||||I have it in mine, and so far no problems. Knock on wood... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||LZeitgeist||11-16-2001||11:46 PM||||Cool.. thanks for the info, guys... I may be looking at the Centerforce instead, although I've heard a couple people say they'd steer clear of the Centerforce clutches, too... *sigh*<P>Dang... why can't this be easy? ::wry smile::||||reg||1
Z||000011||Dan87GT||11-17-2001||06:58 AM||||I put in a Kevlar Zoom clutch, it Kevlar and I got it at AutoZone!! I belive it was listed under heavyduty in the book. I am very happy with it..Just another option||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||11-17-2001||08:04 AM||||I hate to make you continue your search, I just wanted you to know what my experience has been. I've gotten 30K mechanically trouble free miles from it, and I'm sure it has many more left. The stocker I replaced it with, had 150K miles, and it could still light-em-up. I replaced it because I didn't know how much longer it would last, and I didn't want it going out while I was away at school. Turns out there was still at least 50% material left, easily. But the springs had lost all pressure, and it was obviously a high-mileage clutch.<P>I've been recommended the stock replacement from GM. I don't have any complaints with theirs. My Centerforce for the whole set was just over $300. No experience with it yet, but will soon. The pressure plate is stamped with "DK", the disk has the "LUK" stamping.||||reg||1
Z||000013||LZeitgeist||11-17-2001||09:06 AM||||fiero56 - No problem at all - I *do* need to hear the positives and negatives, and so I'm really glad you posted with your experiences... I just did this thing a year ago, never wanting to have to do it again, and the clutch I got was a nameless P.O.S. from Advance Auto, but I had to get something right then to get the car running, so I went for it - and here I am a year later, doing it all over again. Such is luck..<P>This is exactly the kind of info I need, so please don't feel bad - everybody's input is helping a lot. <P>Thanks...||||reg||1
Z||000014||LZeitgeist||11-17-2001||09:09 AM||||Dan - that sounds like a possibility - is Zoom the brand name? Anyone else heard of or used that clutch? <P>I'm hesitant to go back to Advance Auto and get something else that's off-the-shelf, 'cuz I got screwed last time, but I'm open to suggestions from everybody...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||LZeitgeist||11-17-2001||11:54 AM||||O.K. - after some further reading, now I think I'm leaning away from the RAM clutches (due to repeated binding problems I've read about with regard to the center of the clutch disk) and instead I'm considering following Rodney Dickman's lead for the Centerforce...<P>Has anybody got the make/model/numbers for the proper Centerforce to use? Any good deals on price for this clutch? Rodney, can you offer more info?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Rodney||11-17-2001||12:15 PM||||I got my Centerforce at a local speed shop. I never asked the price. It was the one I wanted no matter what. Maybe look at Jegs and Summit? Mine is the top of the line (dual fricton?). Make sure you also get the throwout bearing. Centerforce tries to supply the best clutches and their thowout bearing may be something others offer but it should be the best one available. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||11-17-2001||12:24 PM||||Centerforce makes two clutches for our cars. The Dual Friction, and "I". I bought the C/F I clutch set. I don't think I will need anything more than that.<P>I bought mine from Summit:<BR>These are the part numbers from my receipts, so I don't guarantee these match with C/F.<P>MS361914 - Disk and Plate: $225<BR>N1747 - T.O.B. - $54<P>If you go to the CenterForce website, they have an online catalog with part numbers, and descriptions of each clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000018||LZeitgeist||11-17-2001||01:29 PM||||Excellent! Thanks, guys... I really appreciate your input. <P>Gonna get this nailed down soon...||||reg||1
Z||000019||2birds||11-17-2001||09:25 PM||||My first clutch job happened at 60k miles.  Paid a GM dealer almost $800 to do it, back in 90 or 91.  That one started slipping at 120k miles.  I bought the Ram HD clutch (non-kevlar) from Summitt Racing for $140 (complete kit).  Funny part was, it had the exact same parts (LUK pressure plate and disc) that the GM dealer had put in 4-5 years before then (with a signifcantly higher markup on the "Genuine GM" parts).  I replaced that one after 50k miles, august of 2000, with a no-name brand from a local parts supplier, for about $120/kit.  Mine's a stock 2.8/Getrag GT, but I thrash it hard continuously.  No one clutch has felt any different than the others, and I figure 50-60k is the norm for my driving style.||||reg||1
Z||000020||LZeitgeist||12-11-2001||12:06 PM||||O.K. - just an update to this thread - <P>I got the special Centerforce Dual-Friction clutch that V8 Archie sells, and a GM/Delco OEM throwout bearing (per Archie's recommendation)... and it's all going in the car this weekend.<P>I will try to take the time to photograph the disassembly and reassembly procedures for those who haven't done this yet... me, it'll be the 3rd time on this car and the 4th time overall for me... and dang it, after this, I never want to see the inside of a Fiero bellhousing again... &lt;bleah&gt;  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000021||Black86SE||12-11-2001||06:40 PM||||Here is my question. I bought an Isuzu 5-speed that came out of an '88 4 cyl. I am putting it in my '86 SE V-6. Now, would I have to get the cluch for the V-6 or the 4 cyl. Also, the same question on the throwout bearing. I have heard a couple of different things about the throwout bearing. Some people have used the V-6 one and it worked fine, others say it didn't work for them. Need the info, thanks.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Black86SEsig_1.gif">  <BR>Black 1986 SE V6<BR>Automatic - 5-speed soon!<BR>17" Motegi MR7 Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<BR>AIM ScreenName: Black86se||||reg||1
Z||000022||GT GT 87||12-12-2001||01:04 AM||||I got a C/F from Archie for my 3.2 project. His are the dual friction type , but his are also "ball bearing"  type that the diaphram pivots on . Alows for lots of clamp with normal pedal pressure . Make sure you use a GM throwout bearing .........||||reg||1
Z||000023||Blacktree||12-12-2001||02:42 AM||||Sorry for the thread-jacking, but...<P>I have the same question as <B>Black86SE</B>. I'm going to be transplanting a 1986 2.8 V6 into a 1986 coupe with the Isuzu 5-spd and was wondering if I could use the existing flywheel, plate, t/o bearing, and disc from the 4-banger that's being pulled out. All the above parts (except the flywheel) are relatively new, so I could stand to save some cash if I could re-use them.||||reg||1
Z||000024||tesmith66||12-12-2001||07:56 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black86SE:<BR><B>Here is my question. I bought an Isuzu 5-speed that came out of an '88 4 cyl. I am putting it in my '86 SE V-6. Now, would I have to get the cluch for the V-6 or the 4 cyl. Also, the same question on the throwout bearing. I have heard a couple of different things about the throwout bearing. Some people have used the V-6 one and it worked fine, others say it didn't work for them. Need the info, thanks.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I did the same swap in my 86 SE V6 (black as well). I used the clutch and pressure plate for the Getrag, and the T/O brg for the Isuzu. Use the V6 flywheel, but don't forget to have it surfaced. You'll need the shifter and cables from any Isuzu 5 speed car. The slave cylinder will work on the Isuzu bracket. I got all the parts at Autozone and have had no problems.<P>Be sure to replace the rear main seal and the gasket on the rear camshaft cover while your in there. Cheap insurance.<P>One last thing- the reverse light wiring is already back there with the VSS connector. It's the shabby looking connector that has been dangling in the breeze since 1986. Either add some wire to it or pull it from the conduit and route it over to the pigtail on the tranny.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe<p>[This message has been edited by tesmith66 (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Black86SE||12-12-2001||09:14 PM||||Thanks Tim.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Black86SEsig_1.gif">  <BR>Black 1986 SE V6<BR>Automatic - 5-speed soon!<BR>17" Motegi MR7 Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<BR>AIM ScreenName: Black86se||||reg||1
Z||000026||FieroJoe||12-12-2001||11:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tesmith66:<BR><B>One last thing- the reverse light wiring is already back there with the VSS connector. It's the shabby looking connector that has been dangling in the breeze since 1986. Either add some wire to it or pull it from the conduit and route it over to the pigtail on the tranny.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So, you had an auto and put in the manual trans, and within the engine wiring harness already present, you found the backup light connector?  You kept your original automatic wiring?  What did you do with the enourmous connector on the auto trans that (appears) to have some starter wires on it, seeing as there is a big ole purple on in there too...<P>Asking this because I am helping Black86SE with this swap.||||reg||1
Z||000027||tesmith66||12-13-2001||05:14 PM||||Mine was an original 4 speed. The connector just hung there ever since I got the car.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000028||RobertVol||12-13-2001||10:03 PM||||Well I purchased the Centerforce Clutch Dual Frictiion from Archie....<P>I have not got through the break (seating) period but I will say that the pedal effort is very easy and I like the shifting.. I think the CF clutch is a great clutch. I find actually easier to shift with this clutch than the OEM but mine was bad so the change back to good clutch may be so great that it SEEMS that good... <P>But I can say that the shifting action and peddle pressure is so much easier!||||reg||1
Z||000029||Jimmy||12-13-2001||10:57 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.performanceintl.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.performanceintl.com</A>   is a great site for centerforce clutches.  CF I is $197 and the DF is $252.  I ordered the DF from them and I was very pleased with their service.  The T.O. bearing is not included in the above prices.<P>Jim <BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000030||stingant0||12-13-2001||11:36 PM||||I don't get it? I have the ram clutch in my car and like it A LOT!!! I have had no problems with it whatsoever!! I can take it to 4000 and dump it, it hooks right up, lots of wheelspin. I do have a promblem when i shift into 2nd though (when im at 3500 or so) It seems like synchros but I think it is clutch drag. But I bought the car with the ram clutch so I really can't tell a difference.||||reg||1
